    <!-- Formula Derivation Accordion -->
    <details class="note tip"><summary><span class="lb">Derivation</span> How we derive the Bernoulli Mean & Variance</summary><div class="body">
      <p>By definition of expectation for a discrete random variable:</p>
      \[E(X) = \sum x P(X=x) = 0 \cdot P(X=0) + 1 \cdot P(X=1) = 0(1-p) + 1(p) = p\]
      <p>For the variance, we first find \(E(X^2)\):</p>
      \[E(X^2) = \sum x^2 P(X=x) = 0^2(1-p) + 1^2(p) = p\]
      <p>Then applying \(\operatorname{Var}(X) = E(X^2) - [E(X)]^2\):</p>
      \[\operatorname{Var}(X) = p - p^2 = p(1-p)\]
    </div></details>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.1 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex101" data-label="Example 10.1" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex101">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.1</span><h3>Find the mean and variance of a Bernoulli distribution</h3></div>
        <p>One card is chosen at random from a pack of 52 playing cards (without jokers). Let \(X\) be the random variable representing the number of spades obtained.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>Represent the probability distribution of \(X\) by (i) a table, (ii) a formula, (iii) a bar chart.</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>Find the mean and variance of \(X\).</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex101"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>\(X\) has only two possible values: 0 and 1. \(X\) follows a Bernoulli distribution with the probability of success equal to \(P(\text{A spade})\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>A single draw with two mutually exclusive outcomes is a Bernoulli trial.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[P(X=1) = \frac{13}{52} = \frac{1}{4}, \quad P(X=0) = 1 - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{3}{4}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Identify the parameter \(p\) and \(1-p\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a)(i) The probability distribution of \(X\) is as follows:</p><div class="dtwrap"><table class="dt"><tr><th>\(x\)</th><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><th>\(P(X=x)\)</th><td>\(3/4\)</td><td>\(1/4\)</td></tr></table></div></div><div class="why"><div>All probabilities are non-negative and sum to 1: \(3/4 + 1/4 = 1\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a)(ii) \(P(X=x) = \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^x \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^{1-x}\) for \(x=0, 1\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>Always specify the domain "\(x=0, 1\)" to make the formula complete.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(b)}\quad \text{Mean of } X = p = E(X) = \underline{\frac{1}{4}}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Use the derived property \(E(X) = p\) directly.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{Variance of } X = p(1-p) = \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)\left(\frac{3}{4}\right) = \underline{\frac{3}{16}}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Use the derived property \(\operatorname{Var}(X) = p(1-p)\).</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.2 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex102" data-label="Example 10.2" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex102">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.2</span><h3>Find the parameter, E(X) and Var(X) from a given Bernoulli distribution</h3></div>
        <p>The probability distribution of a Bernoulli variable \(X\) is given by \(P(X=x) = k^x (0.27)^{1-x}\) for \(x=0, 1\).</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>Find the value of \(k\).</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>Find the mean and variance of \(X\).</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex102"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(a)}\quad P(X=0) = 0.27, \quad P(X=1) = k\]</div><div class="why"><div>Substitute \(x=0\) and \(x=1\) into the formula. Any non-zero base raised to the power of 0 equals 1.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[0.27 + k = 1 \implies k = \underline{0.73}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Probabilities over the entire sample space must sum to 1. Thus \(p = 0.73\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) From (a), the probability distribution of \(X\) is \(P(X=x) = (0.73)^x(0.27)^{1-x}\) for \(x=0, 1\).</p>\[\text{Mean of } X = p = \underline{0.73}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Mean equals \(p\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{Variance of } X = p(1-p) = (0.73)(0.27) = \underline{0.1971}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Variance equals \(p(1-p)\).</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

