          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>Let \(X_1\) be the number of defective items in the first sample and \(X_2\) be the number of defective items in the second sample, then</p>\[X_1 \sim B(3, 0.05) \quad \text{and} \quad X_2 \sim B(4, 0.05)\]</div><div class="why"><div>Define the two independent binomial distributions.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(a)}\quad \text{Required probability } = P(X_1=0) = (0.95)^3 = \underline{0.8574} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>First stage passes immediately if all 3 items are good (top branch).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(b)}\quad \text{Required probability } = P(X_1=1) = C_1^3(0.05)(0.95)^2 = \underline{0.1354} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Resampling trigger condition (middle branch).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(c) Required probability</p>\[= P(\text{The result of the first sample is satisfactory})\]\[+ P(\text{Re-sampling is needed and the result of the second sample is satisfactory})\]</div><div class="why"><div>Apply the Law of Total Probability for the two pathways to a satisfactory result.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= P(X_1=0) + P(X_1=1) \times P(X_2 \le 1)\]</div><div class="why"><div>The result of the 1st sample is independent of the result of the 2nd sample.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= (0.95)^3 + C_1^3(0.05)(0.95)^2 \times [(0.95)^4 + C_1^4(0.05)(0.95)^3]\]\[= \underline{0.9909} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Carry full precision in intermediate steps to maintain accuracy.</div></div></li>
