  <!-- Example 10.8 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex108" data-label="Example 10.8" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex108">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.8</span><h3>Problem involving binomial distributions</h3></div>
        <p>In each of the 5 working days of a week, Ryan has a probability of 0.25 to be stuck in a traffic jam. A week is said to be a poor traffic week to him if he is stuck in a traffic jam for more than 3 working days in the week. Assume that being stuck in traffic jams is independent among the working days.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>(i) Find the probability that a particular week is a poor traffic week to Ryan.<br>(ii) Find the expected number of working days that Ryan is stuck in a traffic jam in a week.<br>(iii) Find the expected number of working days that Ryan is stuck in a traffic jam in 5 weeks.</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>A month is said to be an unpleasant month to Ryan if there are at least 2 poor traffic weeks in that month. Assume that there are 4 weeks in next month.<br>(i) Find the probability that next month will be an unpleasant month to Ryan.<br>(ii) Find the expected number of poor traffic weeks to Ryan in next month.<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places if necessary.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex108"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Let \(X\) be the number of working days in a week that Ryan is stuck in a traffic jam, then \(X \sim B(5, 0.25)\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>Establish the base binomial distribution for a single week.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(i)}\quad P(\text{A poor traffic week}) = P(X > 3) = P(X=4) + P(X=5)\]\[= C_4^5 (0.25)^4(0.75) + (0.25)^5 = \underline{0.0156} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>"More than 3" means 4 or 5.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(ii)}\quad \text{Expected number of working days... in a week} = E(X) = 5(0.25) = \underline{1.25}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Apply \(E(X) = np\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(iii) Number of working days in 5 weeks = \(5 \times 5 = 25\).</p>\[\text{Expected number of working days in 5 weeks...} = 25(0.25) = \underline{6.25}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Expectation scales linearly.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Let \(Y\) be the number of poor traffic weeks in next month, then \(Y \sim B(4, p)\), where \(p = P(X > 3) = 0.015625\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>A new Binomial variable where the trial outcome is whether a week was poor.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(i)}\quad P(\text{An unpleasant month}) = P(Y \ge 2) = P(Y=2) + P(Y=3) + P(Y=4)\]\[= C_2^4(0.015625)^2(0.984375)^2 + C_3^4(0.015625)^3(0.984375) + (0.015625)^4 = \underline{0.0014} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Apply the Binomial formula to the new variable \(Y\). Use the exact value of \(p\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(ii)}\quad \text{Expected number of poor traffic weeks in next month} = E(Y) = 4(0.015625) = \underline{0.0625}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Apply \(E(Y) = np\) for the month.</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.9 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex109" data-label="Example 10.9" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex109">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.9</span><h3>Problem involving a binomial distribution</h3></div>
        <p>In each throw of an unfair dice, the probability of obtaining a '6' is 1/9. Suppose James throws the dice \(n\) times.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>Find the least value of \(n\) such that the probability of obtaining at least one '6' is greater than 0.9.</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>For the value of \(n\) obtained in (a), find the probability of obtaining at most two '6's. (Give your answer correct to 4 decimal places.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex109"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Let \(X\) be the number of '6's obtained, then \(X \sim B(n, 1/9)\).</p>\[P(X \ge 1) > 0.9 \implies 1 - P(X=0) > 0.9\]</div><div class="why"><div>Set up the inequality based on the question. "At least one" is the complement of "none".</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[1 - \left(\frac{8}{9}\right)^n > 0.9 \implies \left(\frac{8}{9}\right)^n < 0.1\]</div><div class="why"><div>Substitute \(P(X=0)\). Then isolate the term with \(n\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[n \log\left(\frac{8}{9}\right) < \log 0.1\]</div><div class="why"><div>Take the logarithm of both sides. Bring down the exponent \(n\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[n > \frac{\log 0.1}{\log(8/9)}\]\[n > 19.55 \text{ (cor. to 2 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Since \(\log(8/9) < 0\), the inequality sign <strong>must flip</strong> when you divide by it.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>The least value of \(n\) is <u>20</u>.</p></div><div class="why"><div>Since \(n\) must be an integer, take the next whole number above 19.55.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Required probability = \(P(X \le 2)\)</p>\[= P(X=0) + P(X=1) + P(X=2)\]</div><div class="why"><div>Using \(X \sim B(20, 1/9)\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= \left(\frac{8}{9}\right)^{20} + C_1^{20}\left(\frac{1}{9}\right)\left(\frac{8}{9}\right)^{19} + C_2^{20}\left(\frac{1}{9}\right)^2\left(\frac{8}{9}\right)^{18} = \underline{0.6134} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Sum terms for \(x=0, 1, 2\).</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.10 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex1010" data-label="Example 10.10" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex1010">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.10</span><h3>Problem involving a binomial distribution</h3></div>
        <p>7% of the items manufactured by a production line are defective. The quality manager draws a number of items randomly from the production line and inspects them one by one.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>Find the probability that exactly 3 defective items are found among the first 11 items inspected.</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>Find the probability that the 12th item inspected is the 4th defective item.<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex1010"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Let \(X\) be the number of defective items found out of 11 items inspected, then \(X \sim B(11, 0.07)\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>Define the binomial distribution for the first 11 items.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{Required probability } = P(X=3) = C_3^{11} (0.07)^3 (0.93)^8 = \underline{0.0317} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Standard binomial probability.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Getting exactly 3 defective items in the 1st to 11th items, AND the 12th item is the 4th defective item.</p></div><div class="why"><div>Event decomposition based on the sequence of inspections.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{Required probability } = P(X=3) \times 0.07 \approx 0.031670 \times 0.07 = \underline{0.0022} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Multiply the probability from (a) by the independent probability of the 12th item being defective.</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.11 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex1011" data-label="Example 10.11" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex1011">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.11</span><h3>Problem involving a binomial distribution</h3></div>
        <p>The probability of having a sunny day is 0.65. It is known that the weather in any particular day is independent of the weather in the other days. Assume that Monday is the first day of a week.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>Find the probability that there are exactly 5 sunny days and the 4th one appears after Friday in a certain week.</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>Given that there are at least 5 sunny days in a certain week, find the probability that the 4th sunny day appears after Friday in that week.<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex1011"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Exactly 3 sunny days from Mon to Fri, AND the 4th sunny day is on Sat, and 5th on Sun.</p><p>Let \(X\) be the number of sunny days among the first five days in a week, then \(X \sim B(5, 0.65)\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>If the 4th sunny day is after Friday, exactly 3 sunny days occur Mon–Fri, and both Sat and Sun must be sunny.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{Required probability } = P(X=3) \times (0.65)^2 = C_3^5 (0.65)^3(0.35)^2 \times (0.65)^2 = \underline{0.1421} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Multiply the binomial probability for the 5 weekdays by the probability of 2 sunny weekend days.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Let \(W\) be the number of sunny days in a week, then \(W \sim B(7, 0.65)\).</p>\[P(\text{At least 5 sunny days in a week}) = P(W \ge 5) = P(W=5) + P(W=6) + P(W=7)\]</div><div class="why"><div>Define the distribution for the full week to find the denominator for the conditional probability.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= C_5^7(0.65)^5(0.35)^2 + C_6^7(0.65)^6(0.35) + (0.65)^7 = 0.532283 \text{ (cor. to 6 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Calculate the total probability of the given condition.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{Required probability } = \frac{P(X=3) \times (0.65)^2}{P(W \ge 5)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Apply conditional formula \(P(A \mid B) = P(A \cap B)/P(B)\). The intersection is exactly the event from part (a).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\approx \frac{0.142136}{0.532283} = \underline{0.2670} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Use unrounded values for the final division.</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

