<!-- ===================== POISSON ===================== -->
<section class="view" id="v-pois" data-title="Poisson Distribution Po(λ)" hidden>
  <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">Section 10.2</p><h1>Poisson Distribution <i>Po</i>(&lambda;)</h1></div>

  <div class="page" data-key="idea" data-label="Concepts">
    <p>Models the number of random, independent events in a continuous interval of time or region of space:</p>
    <div class="defn">
      <span class="lbl">Poisson Probability Function</span>
      \[P(X=x) = \frac{e^{-\lambda} \lambda^x}{x!} \quad \text{for } x = 0, 1, 2, \dots\]
      <hr style="border:0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin:8px 0">
      <p><strong>Mean and Variance:</strong></p>
      \[\mu = E(X) = \lambda \qquad\text{and}\qquad \sigma^2 = \operatorname{Var}(X) = \lambda\]
      <p>Notice that for a Poisson distribution, <strong>variance always equals the mean</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- The 3 Poisson Assumptions Illustrated with Petrol Station -->
    <div class="lab">
      <div class="lab-h"><span class="tag teal">Experiment Preview</span><h3>The 3 Poisson Assumptions (Petrol Station Example)</h3></div>
      <p class="small muted">Cars arrive at a petrol station at an average of \(\lambda = 3\) cars per minute. A random variable \(X\) is Poisson if and only if all three assumptions hold:</p>
      
      <div class="svgwrap">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 500 130" id="svgPetrolStation">
          <line class="axis" x1="30" x2="470" y1="60" y2="60"/>
          <text x="30" y="80" class="lbl-s">0s</text>
          <text x="250" y="80" class="lbl-s" text-anchor="middle">30s</text>
          <text x="470" y="80" class="lbl-s" text-anchor="end">60s (1 min)</text>
          
          <!-- Sample car arrival points -->
          <circle cx="110" cy="60" r="6" fill="var(--violet)"/>
          <text x="110" y="45" class="lbl-s" text-anchor="middle">🚗 Car 1</text>
          
          <circle cx="280" cy="60" r="6" fill="var(--violet)"/>
          <text x="280" y="45" class="lbl-s" text-anchor="middle">🚗 Car 2</text>
          
          <circle cx="390" cy="60" r="6" fill="var(--violet)"/>
          <text x="390" y="45" class="lbl-s" text-anchor="middle">🚗 Car 3</text>
          
          <text x="250" y="115" class="lbl-s" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--teal)">Average Rate: λ = 3 cars / minute</text>
        </svg>
      </div>

      <div class="grid2">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>(a) Singularity (At an instant)</h4>
          <p>At most one event can occur at any instantaneous point in time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>(b) Independence</h4>
          <p>Arrivals in any two non-overlapping intervals are completely independent.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="guide-card">
        <h4>(c) Proportionality</h4>
        <p>In any time interval, the expected occurrences are strictly proportional to the length of the interval (e.g., 3 cars in 1 min \(\implies\) 6 cars in 2 mins).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Derivation & Proof that Sum P = 1 -->
    <details class="note tip"><summary><span class="lb">Derivation</span> Proof that all Poisson Probabilities sum to 1</summary><div class="body">
      <p>Using the Taylor expansion of \(e^\lambda = \sum_{x=0}^\infty \frac{\lambda^x}{x!} = 1 + \lambda + \frac{\lambda^2}{2!} + \dots\):</p>
      \[\sum_{x=0}^\infty P(X=x) = \sum_{x=0}^\infty \frac{e^{-\lambda}\lambda^x}{x!} = e^{-\lambda} \sum_{x=0}^\infty \frac{\lambda^x}{x!} = e^{-\lambda} \cdot e^\lambda = e^0 = 1\]
      <p>This confirms that \(P(X=x)\) satisfies both conditions for a valid discrete probability distribution!</p>
    </div></details>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.12 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex1012" data-label="Example 10.12" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex1012">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.12</span><h3>Find probabilities, E(X) and Var(X) from a given Poisson distribution</h3></div>
        <p>It is given that a random variable \(X \sim Po(4.5)\). Find</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>\(P(X = 7)\),</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>\(P(X < 2)\),</span></li>
          <li><b>(c)</b><span>\(E(X)\) and \(\operatorname{Var}(X)\)<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places if necessary.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex1012"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>\(X \sim Po(4.5) \implies P(X=x) = \frac{e^{-4.5}(4.5)^x}{x!}\) for \(x = 0, 1, 2, \dots\)</p></div><div class="why"><div>Identify the probability function with \(\lambda = 4.5\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(a)}\quad P(X=7) = \frac{e^{-4.5}(4.5)^7}{7!} = \underline{0.0824} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Substitute \(x = 7\) into the Poisson formula.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(b)}\quad P(X < 2) = P(X=0) + P(X=1) = \frac{e^{-4.5}(4.5)^0}{0!} + \frac{e^{-4.5}(4.5)^1}{1!} = \underline{0.0611} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Strict inequality \(< 2\) includes only \(x=0\) and \(x=1\). Note that \(0! = 1\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(c)}\quad E(X) = \lambda = \underline{4.5}\]\[\operatorname{Var}(X) = \lambda = \underline{4.5}\]</div><div class="why"><div>In a Poisson distribution, both mean and variance equal the parameter \(\lambda\).</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.13 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex1013" data-label="Example 10.13" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex1013">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.13</span><h3>Problem involving Poisson distributions</h3></div>
        <p>The number of telephone calls coming into an inquiry hotline in a minute follows a Poisson distribution with a mean of 0.2.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>Find the probabilities that, in the next minute,<br>(i) no calls come into the hotline,<br>(ii) exactly 2 calls come into the hotline.</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>Find the probabilities that, in a 15-minute interval,<br>(i) exactly 1 call comes into the hotline,<br>(ii) at least 6 calls come into the hotline.<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex1013"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Let \(X\) be the number of calls coming into the hotline in the next minute, then \(X \sim Po(0.2)\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>Identify the random variable and its parameter \(\lambda\) for a 1-minute interval.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(i)}\quad \text{Required probability } = P(X=0) = e^{-0.2} = \underline{0.8187} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Base 1-minute rate calculation.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(ii)}\quad \text{Required probability } = P(X=2) = \frac{e^{-0.2}(0.2)^2}{2!} = \underline{0.0164} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Substitute \(x = 2\) into the formula.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Mean number of calls coming into the hotline in 15 minutes \(= 0.2 \times 15 = 3\).</p><p>Let \(Y\) be the number of calls coming into the hotline in 15 minutes, then \(Y \sim Po(3)\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>The average number of occurrences is proportional to the size of the interval. Scale \(\lambda\) up by 15.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(i)}\quad \text{Required probability } = P(Y=1) = \frac{e^{-3}3^1}{1!} = \underline{0.1494} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Use the new parameter \(\lambda = 3\) and substitute \(y = 1\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(ii)}\quad \text{Required probability } = P(Y \ge 6) = 1 - P(Y=0) - P(Y=1) - P(Y=2) - P(Y=3) - P(Y=4) - P(Y=5)\]</div><div class="why"><div>Complement rule prevents summing an infinite series.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= 1 - e^{-3} - \frac{e^{-3}3^1}{1!} - \frac{e^{-3}3^2}{2!} - \frac{e^{-3}3^3}{3!} - \frac{e^{-3}3^4}{4!} - \frac{e^{-3}3^5}{5!} = \underline{0.0839} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Calculate the sum of probabilities for 0 to 5 calls and subtract from 1.</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.14 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex1014" data-label="Example 10.14" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex1014">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.14</span><h3>Problem involving Poisson distributions</h3></div>
        <p>Two new albums K and T are available on a music website for members to download. On average, there are 5 downloads of album K and 4 downloads of album T in a day. The number of downloads of each album in a day follows a Poisson distribution and is independent of another album.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>Find the probability that there are exactly 2 downloads of album K and exactly 3 downloads of album T in a certain day.</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>Find the probability that there are a total of 3 downloads of these two albums in a certain day.</span></li>
          <li><b>(c)</b><span>Find the expected total number of downloads of these two albums in a certain day.<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places if necessary.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex1014"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be the numbers of downloads of albums K and T respectively in a day, then \(X \sim Po(5)\) and \(Y \sim Po(4)\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>Define the two independent Poisson distributions.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{Required probability } = P(X=2 \text{ and } Y=3) = P(X=2) \times P(Y=3)\]\[= \frac{e^{-5}5^2}{2!} \times \frac{e^{-4}4^3}{3!} = \underline{0.0165} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>\(X\) and \(Y\) are independent, so multiply their probabilities.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Required probability</p>\[= P(X=3) \times P(Y=0) + P(X=2) \times P(Y=1) + P(X=1) \times P(Y=2) + P(X=0) \times P(Y=3)\]</div><div class="why"><div>Add probabilities of all disjoint combinations where \(X+Y=3\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= \frac{e^{-5}5^3}{3!} \times e^{-4} + \frac{e^{-5}5^2}{2!} \times \frac{e^{-4}4^1}{1!} + \frac{e^{-5}5^1}{1!} \times \frac{e^{-4}4^2}{2!} + e^{-5} \times \frac{e^{-4}4^3}{3!}\]\[= \underline{0.0150} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Evaluate each product and sum them.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[\text{(c)}\quad \text{Expected total number of downloads...} = E(X) + E(Y) = 5 + 4 = \underline{9}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Expectation is always additive: \(E(X+Y) = E(X) + E(Y)\).</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="page" data-key="check" data-label="Check"><div class="check" data-sec="poisson"></div></div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== CHOOSING & MIXED ===================== -->
<section class="view" id="v-mixed" data-title="Choosing & Mixed Models" hidden>
  <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">Section 10.3</p><h1>Choosing an Appropriate Distribution</h1></div>

  <div class="page" data-key="matrix" data-label="Decision Matrix">
    <p>The following table summarizes the characteristics and concepts in different discrete probability distributions we have covered before.</p>
    <div class="dtwrap"><table class="dt">
      <tr><th>Random variable \(X\)</th><th>Parameter(s)</th><th>Probability function of \(X\)</th><th>\(E(X)\)</th><th>\(\operatorname{Var}(X)\)</th></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align:left"><strong>Bernoulli variable:</strong><br>It takes 1 for a 'success' and 0 for a 'failure' in a trial</td><td>\(p\): the probability of success in a trial</td><td>\(P(X=x) = p^x(1-p)^{1-x}\) for \(x=0, 1\)</td><td>\(p\)</td><td>\(p(1-p)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align:left"><strong>Binomial variable:</strong><br>Number of successes in \(n\) identical and independent Bernoulli trials</td><td>\(p\): the probability of success in each trial<br>\(n\): the total number of trials</td><td>\(P(X=x) = C_x^n p^x(1-p)^{n-x}\) for \(x=0, 1, 2, \dots, n\)</td><td>\(np\)</td><td>\(np(1-p)\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td style="text-align:left"><strong>Poisson variable:</strong><br>Number of occurrences of an event in an interval of time or a region of space</td><td>\(\lambda\): the average number of occurrences of the event in a specified interval of time or region of space</td><td>\(P(X=x) = \frac{e^{-\lambda}\lambda^x}{x!}\) for \(x=0, 1, 2, \dots\)</td><td>\(\lambda\)</td><td>\(\lambda\)</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.15 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex1015" data-label="Example 10.15" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex1015">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.15</span><h3>Find probabilities involving more than one discrete probability distribution</h3></div>
        <p>There are 5.2 calls received by Jay each day on average, and the number of calls received per day follows a Poisson distribution. It is found that 60% of the calls are marketing calls and the occurrences of the marketing calls are independent of each other. Find the probabilities that, in a particular day,</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>more than 3 calls are received by Jay,</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>exactly 6 calls are received by Jay and exactly 5 of them are marketing calls,</span></li>
          <li><b>(c)</b><span>fewer than 4 calls are received by Jay and at least 1 of them is a marketing call.<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex1015"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Let \(X\) be the number of calls received by Jay in a day, then \(X \sim Po(5.2)\).</p>\[\text{Required probability } = P(X > 3) = 1 - e^{-5.2} - \frac{e^{-5.2}(5.2)^1}{1!} - \frac{e^{-5.2}(5.2)^2}{2!} - \frac{e^{-5.2}(5.2)^3}{3!}\]\[= \underline{0.7619} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Standard Poisson complement calculation.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Let \(Y\) be the number of marketing calls received out of 6 calls, then \(Y \sim B(6, 0.6)\).</p>\[\text{Required probability } = P(X=6 \text{ and } Y=5) = P(X=6) \times P(Y=5)\]</div><div class="why"><div>Layer a Binomial trial on top of a Poisson volume.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= \frac{e^{-5.2}(5.2)^6}{6!} \times C_5^6(0.6)^5(0.4) = \underline{0.0283} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Calculate the product of the two probabilities.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(c) If no calls are received by Jay, it is impossible for him to receive at least 1 marketing call. Thus, \(P(X=0 \text{ and at least 1 marketing call}) = 0\).</p><p>Let \(W_i\) be the number of marketing calls received out of \(i\) calls where \(i > 0\), then \(W_i \sim B(i, 0.6)\).</p></div><div class="why"><div>Condition on total calls \(X \in \{1, 2, 3\}\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>Required probability</p>\[= P(X=1) \times P(W_1 \ge 1) + P(X=2) \times P(W_2 \ge 1) + P(X=3) \times P(W_3 \ge 1)\]</div><div class="why"><div>Sum the probabilities of these mutually exclusive pathways. Note \(P(W_1 \ge 1) = P(W_1 = 1) = 0.6\) and \(P(W_3 \ge 1) = 1 - P(W_3 = 0) = 1 - (0.4)^3\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= \frac{e^{-5.2}(5.2)^1}{1!} \times 0.6 + \frac{e^{-5.2}(5.2)^2}{2!} \times [C_1^2(0.6)(0.4) + (0.6)^2] + \frac{e^{-5.2}(5.2)^3}{3!} \times [1-(0.4)^3]\]\[= \underline{0.2009} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Calculate and sum the terms.</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Example 10.16 -->
  <div class="page" data-key="ex1016" data-label="Example 10.16" data-ex="1">
    <div class="player" data-viz="ex1016">
      <div class="pq">
        <div class="qh"><span class="tag amber">Example 10.16</span><h3>Find probabilities involving more than one discrete probability distribution</h3></div>
        <p>The number of road accidents occurring at a junction follows a Poisson distribution and road accidents occur with a mean rate of 2 per week.</p>
        <ol class="parts">
          <li><b>(a)</b><span>What is the probability that no road accidents occur at the junction in a particular week?</span></li>
          <li><b>(b)</b><span>What is the probability that, in 5 consecutive weeks, there are exactly 3 weeks with no road accidents occurred at the junction?</span></li>
          <li><b>(c)</b><span>In 5 consecutive weeks, what is the expected number of weeks with no road accidents occurred at the junction?</span></li>
          <li><b>(d)</b><span>What is the probability that, in 6 consecutive weeks, the last week is the third one with no road accidents occurred at the junction?</span></li>
          <li><b>(e)</b><span>What is the probability that, in 5 consecutive weeks, there are exactly 2 weeks with no road accidents occurred at the junction and a total of 6 road accidents occur in these 5 weeks?<br>(Give your answers correct to 4 decimal places.)</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="pmain">
        <div class="pviz"><svg viewBox="0 0 320 200" id="svg-ex1016"></svg></div>
        <div>
          <p class="pempty">Try it on paper first, then press Start ▶</p>
          <ol class="pwork">
            <li><div class="w"><p>(a) Let \(X\) be the number of road accidents occurring in a week, then \(X \sim Po(2)\).</p>\[\text{Required probability } = P(X=0) = e^{-2} = \underline{0.1353} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Weekly base rate calculation.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(b) Let \(Y\) be the number of weeks with no road accidents out of 5 weeks, then \(Y \sim B(5, e^{-2})\).</p>\[\text{Required probability } = P(Y=3) = C_3^5 (e^{-2})^3 (1-e^{-2})^2 = \underline{0.0185} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Treating each week as an independent trial, creating a binomial distribution. \(P(\text{No road accidents occurring in a week}) = P(X=0) = e^{-2}\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(c) Expected number of weeks with no road accidents out of 5 weeks</p>\[= E(Y) = 5(e^{-2}) = \underline{0.6767} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Use the expected value formula for the binomial distribution \(Y\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(d) Required probability \(= P(Y=2) \times e^{-2}\)</p>\[= C_2^5 (e^{-2})^2 (1-e^{-2})^3 \times e^{-2} = \underline{0.0160} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>The 6th week must be accident-free, preceded by exactly 2 in the first 5.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>(e) Possible number of road accidents per week (out of the 3 weeks with accidents):</p>
              <p>Combination 1: 0, 0, 1, 1, 4 (Number of ways \(= C_2^5 C_2^3\))</p>
              <p>Combination 2: 0, 0, 1, 2, 3 (Number of ways \(= C_2^5 C_1^3 C_1^2 = P_3^5\))</p>
              <p>Combination 3: 0, 0, 2, 2, 2 (Number of ways \(= C_2^5\))</p>
            </div><div class="why"><div>Partition 6 accidents into the 3 non-zero weeks. Note \(P_3^5 = \frac{5!}{(5-3)!}\).</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w"><p>Required probability \(= P(Y=2 \text{ and a total of 6 road accidents occur})\)</p>
              \[= C_2^5 C_2^3 [P(X=0)]^2 [P(X=1)]^2 [P(X=4)] + P_3^5 [P(X=0)]^2 [P(X=1)][P(X=2)][P(X=3)] + C_2^5 [P(X=0)]^2 [P(X=2)]^3\]</div><div class="why"><div>Sum the probabilities for all possible combinations.</div></div></li>
            <li><div class="w">\[= C_2^5 C_2^3 (e^{-2})^2 \left[\frac{e^{-2}2^1}{1!}\right]^2 \left(\frac{e^{-2}2^4}{4!}\right) + P_3^5 (e^{-2})^2 \left(\frac{e^{-2}2^1}{1!}\right) \left(\frac{e^{-2}2^2}{2!}\right) \left(\frac{e^{-2}2^3}{3!}\right) + C_2^5 (e^{-2})^2 \left[\frac{e^{-2}2^2}{2!}\right]^3\]\[= \underline{0.0218} \text{ (cor. to 4 d.p.)}\]</div><div class="why"><div>Substitute the Poisson probabilities and evaluate.</div></div></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

